Checking a table file: htr.c and its Capla version, side by side
================================================================

htr.c        Paul Zimmermann's search, in C with GMP and OpenMP; ERR =
             0x600000 is in its source, k and l are read on each line.
htr3.b       the same search for one line, in Capla
             (gitlab.inria.fr/fresco/capla), with k, l and ERR as arguments.
main_htr3.c  C driver of htr3.b (Capla has no input/output): reads a table
             file in htr.c's format, runs the search on each line and prints
             the candidates x0 + j u, one per line, as htr.c does.
htr3_capla.c htr3.b written out as C by Capla (`ccomp -dcaplac`, an
             unverified backend), kept so that the Capla search runs with
             gcc alone.  It is generated code, hard to read (renamed
             variables, a bounds check before each array access): read
             htr3.b.  `make capla-c` regenerates it (needs Capla).

The format.  A table file in<e> holds one line per range of inputs:

  x0 N k l B_00 .. B_0(l-1) B_10 .. B_(k-1)(l-1)

x0 is the first input, N the number of inputs x0 + j u (j = 0 .. N-1, u
the ulp of x0), k the number of coefficients (1 <= k <= 13) and l the
number of 64-bit words of each (1 <= l <= 8); then the k l words of
B_0 .. B_(k-1) in hexadecimal, each B_i least significant word first.
htr.c and main_htr3.c stop on an assertion when k or l is out of these
bounds.  main_htr3.c reads the k l words into one array, B_i at
B[i*l .. i*l + l), as htr3.b expects.

Building.  Three programs:

  htr       htr.c, gcc -O3 -march=native, GMP and OpenMP
  htr3gcc   htr3_capla.c + main_htr3.c, gcc -O2: needs only gcc
  htr3cap   htr3.b + main_htr3.c by Capla's verified compiler (ccomp);
            needs the Capla compiler in ~/git/capla-compiler (CAPLA=...)

`make htr htr3gcc` needs gcc and GMP only; `make` builds all three.
By hand: `./htr FILE` and `./htr3gcc FILE 0x600000` (FILE, then ERR).

Checking one file.  make copies the file it needs into tables/, once,
from the 13 parts of code/exptablekl/archive, whose INDEX says which part
holds it (a few seconds); TABLES=dir (a directory holding the files
in<e>) or TABLES=x.tar.bz2 takes it from elsewhere, ARCHIVE=... from
other parts.

  make in1024.htr              the candidates of htr
  make in1024.htrc             those of the Capla search (htr3gcc;
                                  PROG=htr3cap for the verified build)
  make in1024.cmp              both, and checks they are the same
  make in1024.cmp RANGE=10-15  only lines 10 to 15 of the file
  make in1024.cmp MAXN=1000000 only the first 10^6 inputs of each line

RANGE=a-b keeps lines a to b, numbered from 1; MAXN=n keeps the first n
inputs of each line (only N, the second field, changes; k and l stay).
Without them the whole file is checked; with MAXN the check is partial.
The outputs list every candidate x0 + j u of the lines checked (htr.c
prints them in the order its threads find them, so .cmp sorts both).
Each run is timed: make prints its wall and user time and keeps them in
in1024.htr.time and in1024.htrc.time.  htr.c runs lines in parallel, so
with several lines its wall time is below its user time; compare user
times.  A line with more than 4000000 candidates stops the Capla run.
Without Capla installed, everything above works with the default
PROG=htr3gcc.

Examples with candidates.  Measured on the desktop, one core (taskset -c
0), turbo off; user times of htr / htr3gcc / htr3cap:

  make in1-52.cmp RANGE=1-1 MAXN=100000000         k = 2, l = 2
  same candidates (1536)          0.5 s / 0.6 s / 1.2 s
  make in0-32.cmp RANGE=439-439 MAXN=2146463000    k = 3, l = 2
  same candidates (1)             29.6 s / 24.0 s / 47.2 s
  make in-1074.cmp RANGE=1-1 MAXN=100000000        k = 13, l = 8
  same candidates (3)             10.0 s / 18.6 s / 28.6 s

The line of in1-52 starts at x0 = 2^-53, where exp(x0) is close to the
midpoint 1 + 2^-53: its candidates are its first 1536 inputs,
0x1p-53 .. 0x1.00000000005ffp-53.  The candidate of in0-32,
-0x1.f92bf800f937ap-33 (j = 2146462416), was located first by computing
where exp crosses a midpoint on that line.  Those of in-1074 are its first
three inputs, -0x1.74910d52d3051p+9 .. -0x1.74910d52d304fp+9.  A
candidate is an input whose top word falls in the window; the search
misses no hard-to-round input, but each candidate still needs an exact
check to be one.

The two programs, line by line:

  htr.c                                    htr3.b
  add(a, b, n), sub(a, b, n)               addw, subw: the same statements,
                                           on one array at two offsets
  for i = 1 .. k-1, j = k-1 down to i:     difftab: the same loops
    sub(B[j], B[j-1], l)
  B[0][l-1] += ERR                         B[l-1] = B[l-1] + err
  for i = 0 .. N-1:                        for j = 0 .. n-1:
    if (B[0][l-1] <= 2*ERR) check(x0+i*u)    if (B[l-1] <= 2*err) record j
    for j = 0 .. k-2:                        tstep: for t = 0 .. k-2,
      mpn_add_n(B[j], B[j], B[j+1], l)         addw(t, t+1)

  The step addition is GMP's mpn_add_n in htr.c and the hand-written add in
  htr3.b; both compute B_j + B_(j+1) mod 2^(64 l).  frexp, u = 2^(e-53) and
  x0 + j u are in main_htr3.c, as in htr.c.  htr.c runs lines in parallel
  (OpenMP); main_htr3.c runs them one after the other.

Cost.  A line costs N steps, and a step costs more as k and l grow.  The
tables in code/exptablekl/archive (2203 files, 2931305 lines;
computed): every file has one (k, l) for all its lines; 52 lines have
N = 1; no line has N above 2^38 + 1, and most lines with k <= 8 have
exactly that.
Time per step measured on the desktop over 10^8 steps of one line, one
core, turbo off (a slow clock chosen to compare programs, not to predict a
run); hours per line scaled from it with htr at the mean N:

   k  l    lines   mean N   us/step: htr  htr3gcc  htr3cap   h/line (htr)
   2  2   655380  2.75e11          0.006   0.008    0.019    0.5
   3  2   524304  2.75e11          0.016   0.014    0.026    1.2
   4  3   262152  2.75e11          0.022   0.035    0.047    1.7
   5  4   163845  2.75e11          0.030   0.047    0.070    2.3
   6  4   130514  2.68e11          0.036   0.058    0.087    2.7
   7  5    65588  2.75e11          0.049   0.088    0.118    3.7
   8  5    69919  2.74e11          0.065   0.099    0.144    5.0
   9  6    79628  2.24e11          0.080   0.130    0.184    5.0
  10  7    94210  9.53e10          0.095   0.167    0.218    2.5
  11  7   189442  4.77e10          0.098   0.169    0.235    1.3
  12  8   377858  2.38e10          0.106   0.200    0.315    0.7
  13  8   318465  1.19e10          0.127   0.217    0.391    0.4

The steps of a file: awk '{s += $2} END {print s}' tables/in1024.  Over
the tables, 5.6e17 steps (computed), about 1.4e10 core-seconds with htr at
these speeds (scaled); a faster core divides it.

Tests: htr, htr3gcc and htr3cap give the same candidates on the three
examples above, and on line 2, cut to 10^8 inputs, of in1-52 (k = 2,
l = 2), in1-17 (3, 2), in1-10 (4, 3), in0-4 (5, 4), in0-0 (6, 4), in-5
(7, 5), in-23 (8, 5), in-47 (9, 6), in-100 (10, 7), in-185 (11, 7),
in-370 (12, 8), in-1000 (13, 8) (no candidates there: the times of the
table above).  No whole line has been run.

What is proved is in ../proof: on Capla's semantics, search returns every
j < N that the hypotheses of hscan_exp make hard to round.  Not proved:
htr.c, the C driver main_htr3.c, and that htr.c and htr3.b compute the same.
